  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nMainly dry conditions will continue through tonight. Enough\nbreaks in the clouds could allow for some patchy fog to form\ntonight. Otherwise, it will not be as cool as previous nights\nwith lows in the mid-50s to lower 60s.\n\nA weak area of low pressure will track across the area tomorrow\nwith enough moisture to bring rain showers to much of the area.\nThe latest CAMs show an earlier arrival time of showers \ncompared to previous forecasts with precipitation starting in \nthe morning for areas mainly west of the Hudson Valley and in\nthe afternoon from the Hudson and points east. With the earlier\narrival time of clouds and showers, there will likely be little\nto no instability present, so thunder was mostly removed from \nthe forecast. While precipitable water values will be high on \nthe order of 1.50 to 2.00 inches (+2 to +3 STDEV), the overall \nlimited coverage of heavier, convective showers will likely \nprevent much if any hydrological concerns, though some ponding \nof water in urban and poor drainage areas is possible where any \nrepeated downpours occur. Total QPF amounts look to generally \nrange between 0.10 and 0.50 inches for most areas. Should the \nearlier arrival time trend continues, forecast high temperatures\nwill likely need to be lowered several degrees due to wet-bulb \neffects.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA strong cold front crosses the region Thursday night into early\nFriday as high pressure builds back into the region from Canada.\nThis will bring a cooler, drier and less humid air mass into the\nregion for Friday and Saturday. A chilly night Friday night\ncould lead to some patchy frost in parts of the Adirondacks.\n\nSaturday will likely be the nicer of the two days this weekend\nas confidence continues to increase on a low pressure system\ntracking from the Upper Midwest eastward across the area on\nSunday. Most of the global models, including the EC-AIFS and\nAIGFS, are in general agreement with the timing and track of\nthis system. The region will be located within the right\nentrance region of an upper level jet which will allow for\nplenty of upper level forcing and ascent for a widespread\nrainfall event. Latest NBM probabilities for greater than 1 \ninch of rain Sunday into Monday are between 40 and 60 percent. \nWhile most of this rain should be beneficial, will monitor\ntrends for any possible localized hydrological issues, \nespecially ponding of water in urban and poor drainage areas. If\nthis rain materializes, it could be a rather cool day with some\nareas potentially not getting out of the 50s.\n\nAs this system departs, a frontal boundary will likely stall\nnear or south of our area to start next week. Most global models\nkeep it south enough for high pressure to nose into the area\nbringing drier weather to start the week. However, the chance\nfor some additional showers will not be too far away if this\nboundary ends up a little farther north. Will keep low chances\nfor showers in for early next week due to uncertainty.\nTemperatures early next week will also likely remain slightly\nbelow normal, mainly in the 60s.",
